--- A function called with a map of ints and with a map of floats is an
--- internal error in both modes, before anything runs.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1, nothing printed):
---     runtime_evaluation_in_an_environment-1.co: internal error: the lowered
---     program disagrees with itself — a bug in Crow, not in this program:
---     runtime_evaluation_in_an_environment-1 main: line 27: hands 'show'
---     Map(Int) for its parameter 'm' (argument 1), which is Map(Float)
--- Expected (both modes): the function copied per signature, or the map of
--- ints widened to floats where it is handed (as a list of ints beside a list
--- of floats is):
---     [x = 3]
---     [x = 0.5]
---
--- The same with the calls in the other order, with the maps held in variables
--- first, with maps of two keys, and when the body reads the values
--- (`t = t + m[k]`). Not with lists: `show[[3]]` then `show[[0.5]]` runs and
--- prints [3] and [0.5]; not with a map of text beside a map of ints
--- (`show[map[x = 'a']]`, `show[map[x = 3]]` runs). Found writing
--- Runtime evaluation/In an environment, whose `show[program, before, after]`
--- was handed bindings `map[x = 3]` and, for `exp[x]`, `map[x = 0.0]`; worked
--- around there by binding whole numbers for `exp[x]` too.
fn show[m]
    log[m]

show[map[x = 3]]
show[map[x = 0.5]]
